@cvetaevvitaliy, I've made some additional changes to your code to get the display rotation to work for all three types of displays under test (240x240, 240x320, and 135x240) without making any changes in my applications or the `st7789v devicetree overlays`. 

As suspected, the problem with the code was the x_offset & y_offset for the 90/180 degree rotations.

In  `st7789v_set_orientation` , I've added a test for the display size. 

If the display is 135x240 (width = 135, height = 240), no adjustments of x_offset & y_offset are required as the correct offsets are already specified in the st7789v overlay, i.e. x-offset = 52, y-offset = 40. These values are derived from row_offset = (240 - width)/2 = (240 - 135)/2 = 52 (whole integer) and col_offset = (320 - height)/2 = (320 - 240)/2 = 40. So, no x_offset/y_offset adjustments are required in the code.

If the display size is `240x240 (width = height = 240) and with x-offset = y-offset = 0 in st7789v overlay`, then the required row_offset = (320 - height) = (320 - 240) = 80 and col_offset = (240 - width) = (240 - 240) = 0. This should be handled in the code, as shown below.

If the display size is `240x320 (width = 240, height = 320) and with x-offset = y-offset = 0 in st7789v overlay`, then row_offset = col_offset = 0. There are no x_offset/y_offset adjustments required in the code.

Actually, for all the above display sizes, we could adjust the offsets in the code but I wanted to minimize changes to your code and also not modify my `st7789v device overlays` for the displays.

Below is the tested modifications to your code. Only `st7789v.c` was changed.

When you get hold of a 240x240, please review review & test. It'll be easier for you to incorporate my changes into your code, make any other changes you see fit and submit for review.

```
static int st7789v_set_orientation(const struct device *dev,
			    const enum display_orientation orientation)
{
	struct st7789v_data *data = (struct st7789v_data *)dev->data;
	uint8_t tx_data = 0;
	uint16_t x_offset = 0;
	uint16_t y_offset = 0;

        uint16_t row_offset = 0;
        uint16_t col_offset = 0;

        if (data->width < 240) {
          // 135x240 display
          row_offset = data->y_offset;
          col_offset = data->x_offset;
        }
        else {
          // 240x320 and 240x240 displays
          row_offset = (320 - data->height);
          col_offset = (240 - data->width);
        }

	switch (orientation)
	{
	case DISPLAY_ORIENTATION_NORMAL:
		tx_data |= ST7789V_MADCTL_MV_NORMAL_MODE;
		x_offset = data->x_offset;
		y_offset = data->y_offset;
		break;

	case DISPLAY_ORIENTATION_ROTATED_90:
		tx_data |= (ST7789V_MADCTL_MY_BOTTOM_TO_TOP | ST7789V_MADCTL_MV_REVERSE_MODE);
		x_offset = row_offset;
		y_offset = col_offset;
		break;

	case DISPLAY_ORIENTATION_ROTATED_180:
		tx_data |= (ST7789V_MADCTL_MY_BOTTOM_TO_TOP | ST7789V_MADCTL_MX_RIGHT_TO_LEFT);
		x_offset = col_offset;
		y_offset = row_offset;
		break;

	case DISPLAY_ORIENTATION_ROTATED_270:
		tx_data |= (ST7789V_MADCTL_MX_RIGHT_TO_LEFT | ST7789V_MADCTL_MV_REVERSE_MODE);
		x_offset = data->y_offset;
		y_offset = data->x_offset;
		break;

	default:
		LOG_ERR("Error changing display orientation");
		return -ENOTSUP;
	}

	st7789v_set_lcd_margins(data, x_offset, y_offset);
	st7789v_transmit(data, ST7789V_CMD_MADCTL, &tx_data, 1U);
	data->orientation = orientation;
	LOG_INF("Changing orientation to: '%d'", data->orientation);

	return 0;
}

```

